The table structure information is ({database name: {schema name: [table name]}}): 
{'THELOOK_ECOMMERCE': {'THELOOK_ECOMMERCE': ['DISTRIBUTION_CENTERS', 'ORDERS', 'USERS', 'EVENTS', 'INVENTORY_ITEMS', 'PRODUCTS', 'ORDER_ITEMS']}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• ORDERS."order_id": primary key to identify each order and to join with ORDER_ITEMS.  
• ORDERS."user_id": ties an order back to the customer (USERS)."id".  
• ORDERS."created_at": lets us locate the first order chronologically for every customer.  
• ORDERS."status": helps exclude cancelled orders (condition: "status" <> 'Cancelled').  
• ORDER_ITEMS."order_id": joins an item to its parent order.  
• ORDER_ITEMS."product_id": joins to PRODUCTS."id" so we can see the item’s category.  
• ORDER_ITEMS."sale_price": revenue contributed by that item.  
• ORDER_ITEMS."status": helps exclude items that were returned (condition: "status" <> 'Returned').  
• ORDER_ITEMS."returned_at": an additional‑safety filter for non‑returned items (condition: "returned_at" IS NULL).  
• PRODUCTS."id": primary key that joins to ORDER_ITEMS."product_id".  
• PRODUCTS."category": tells us the product category, critical for grouping by category.  
• USERS."id": the customer identifier (helps double‑check counts of distinct customers).  

Typical conditions we will use  
1. ORDERS."status" <> 'Cancelled'  
2. ORDER_ITEMS."status" <> 'Returned' OR ORDER_ITEMS."returned_at" IS NULL  
3. Keep the first chronologically valid order per customer by MIN("created_at")  

Below are ten Snowflake SQL queries (LIMIT 20 in each) that progress from simple exploration to the final answer‑oriented step.

```sql
--Description: 1) Peek at order statuses just to understand the variety of values.
SELECT DISTINCT "status"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDERS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2) Peek at item‑level statuses to see how returns are tracked.
SELECT DISTINCT "status"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3) Sample of non‑cancelled orders (parent‑level filter).
SELECT "order_id",
       "user_id",
       "status",
       "created_at"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDERS"
WHERE "status" <> 'Cancelled'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 4) Sample of non‑returned order items (item‑level filter).
SELECT "id"           AS "order_item_id",
       "order_id",
       "product_id",
       "status",
       "returned_at"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS"
WHERE "returned_at" IS NULL
  AND "status" <> 'Returned'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 5) Join orders + order_items after applying both filters; look at first few rows.
SELECT o."order_id",
       o."user_id",
       o."created_at",
       i."product_id",
       i."sale_price"
FROM   "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDERS"        o
JOIN   "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS"   i
  ON   o."order_id" = i."order_id"
WHERE  o."status" <> 'Cancelled'
  AND  i."returned_at" IS NULL
  AND  i."status" <> 'Returned'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 6) Derive each customer’s very first *valid* order id (non‑cancelled/non‑returned).
SELECT DISTINCT FIRST_VALUE(o."order_id") OVER (PARTITION BY o."user_id" 
                                                ORDER BY o."created_at") AS "first_good_order_id",
       o."user_id"
FROM   "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDERS"        o
JOIN   "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS"   i
  ON   o."order_id" = i."order_id"
WHERE  o."status" <> 'Cancelled'
  AND  i."returned_at" IS NULL
  AND  i."status" <> 'Returned'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 7) Attach product category to those first good orders.
WITH first_good AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT FIRST_VALUE(o."order_id") OVER (PARTITION BY o."user_id" 
                                                  ORDER BY o."created_at") AS "first_good_order_id",
         o."user_id"
  FROM   "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDERS"      o
  JOIN   "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS" i
    ON   o."order_id" = i."order_id"
  WHERE  o."status" <> 'Cancelled'
    AND  i."returned_at" IS NULL
    AND  i."status" <> 'Returned'
)
SELECT fg."user_id",
       p."category"
FROM   first_good                     fg
JOIN   "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS"   i
  ON   fg."first_good_order_id" = i."order_id"
JOIN   "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."PRODUCTS"      p
  ON   i."product_id" = p."id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 8) Count distinct customers by category for their first good order (top 20 categories).
WITH first_good AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT FIRST_VALUE(o."order_id") OVER (PARTITION BY o."user_id" 
                                                  ORDER BY o."created_at") AS "first_good_order_id",
         o."user_id"
  FROM   "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDERS"      o
  JOIN   "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS" i
    ON   o."order_id" = i."order_id"
  WHERE  o."status" <> 'Cancelled'
    AND  i."returned_at" IS NULL
    AND  i."status" <> 'Returned'
)
SELECT p."category",
       COUNT(DISTINCT fg."user_id") AS "num_customers_first_order"
FROM   first_good                     fg
JOIN   "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS"   i
  ON   fg."first_good_order_id" = i."order_id"
JOIN   "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."PRODUCTS"      p
  ON   i."product_id" = p."id"
GROUP  BY p."category"
ORDER  BY "num_customers_first_order" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

